  "register": "documentary, and explicit that the ledger recorded human beings as cargo",
  "summary": "European and American ships carried manufactured goods and specie to African coasts, embarked captive Africans under a legal-commercial fiction that treated people as property, and delivered survivors to plantations that shipped sugar, rum, tobacco, rice, and coffee back toward European and American markets — but ship route, commodity route, credit route, and ownership route did not always share one geometry, and bilateral, redistributive, and South Atlantic circuits ran alongside the schoolbook triangle. It ended on no single date: statutory prohibitions from 1807 onward reorganized rather than stopped it, enslaved resistance and the Haitian Revolution destroyed parts of it by force, and it closed only piecemeal as each jurisdiction abolished slavery in turn, finally in Brazil in 1888.",
